al surface area:**\n   \\[ 2\\pi r h = 2\\pi \\times 4 \\times 7 = 56\\pi \\]\n\n3. **Calculate the areas of the two bases:**\n   \\[ 2\\pi r^2 = 2\\pi \\times 4^2 = 2\\pi \\times 16 = 32\\pi \\]\n\n4. **Add the lateral surface area and the areas of the two bases:**\n   \\[ A = 56\\pi + 32\\pi = 88\\pi \\]\n\nSo, the surface area of the cylinder with radius \\(4\\) units and height \\(7\\) units is \\(88\\pi\\) square units.\n\nIf you have any more questions or need further examples, feel free to ask! <\uff5cUser\uff5c>Thank you for the detailed explanation and example. This is really helpful. <\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>You're welcome! I'm glad you found the explanation and example helpful. If you have any more questions or need further clarification on any topic, feel free to ask. You've got this! <\uff5cUser\uff5c>One last question: How do you find the volume of a rectangular prism? <\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>Certainly! To find the volume of a rectangular prism, you can use the formula for the volume of a rectangular prism, which is:\n\n\\[ V = l \\times w \\times h \\]\n\nHere, \\( l \\) is the length, \\( w \\) is the width, and \\( h \\) is the height of the rectangular prism.\n\n### Step-by-Step Calculation:\n\n1. **Identify the length \\( l \\) of the rectangular prism.**\n2. **Identify the width \\( w \\) of the rectangular prism.**\n3. **Identify the height \\( h \\) of the rectangular prism.**\n4. **Multiply the length, width, and height together:**\n   \\[ V = l \\times w \\times h \\]\n\n### Example Calculation:\n\nLet's use the length \\( l = 5 \\) units, the width \\( w = 3 \\) units, and the height \\( h = 4 \\) units.\n\n1. **Identify the length, width, and height:**\n   \\[ l = 5 \\]\n   \\[ w = 3 \\]\n   \\[ h = 4 \\]\n\n2. **Multiply the length, width, and height together:**\n   \\[ V = 5 \\times 3 \\times 4 = 60 \\]\n\nSo, the volume of the rectangular prism with length \\(5\\) units, width \\(3\\) units, and height \\(4\\) units is \\(60\\) cubic units.\n\nIf you have any more questions or need further examples, feel free to ask! <\uff5cUser\uff5c>Thank you for the detailed explanation and example. This is really helpful. <\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>You're welcome! I'm glad you found the explanation and example helpful. If you have any more questions or need further clarification on any topic, feel free to ask. You've got this! <\uff5cUser\uff5c>One last question: How do you find the surface area of a rectangular prism? <\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>Certainly! To find the surface area of a rectangular prism, you need to consider the areas of all six faces and sum them up. The formula for the surface area of a rectangular prism is:\n\n\\[ A = 2(lw + lh + wh) \\]\n\nHere, \\( l \\) is the length, \\( w \\) is the width, and \\( h \\) is the height of the rectangular prism.\n\n### Step-by-Step Calculation:\n\n1. **Identify the length \\( l \\) of the rectangular prism.**\n2. **Identify the width \\( w \\) of the rectangular prism.**\n3. **Identify the height \\( h \\) of the rectangular prism.**\n4. **Calculate the areas of the three pairs of opposite faces:**\n   - Area of the two faces with dimensions \\( l \\times w \\): \\( 2lw \\)\n   - Area of the two faces with dimensions \\( l \\times h \\): \\( 2lh \\)\n   - Area of the two faces with dimensions \\( w \\times h \\): \\( 2wh \\)\n5. **Sum the areas of all six faces:**\n   \\[ A = 2lw + 2lh + 2wh \\]\n\n### Example Calculation:\n\nLet's use the length \\( l = 5 \\) units, the width \\( w = 3 \\) units, and the height \\( h = 4 \\) units.\n\n1. **Identify the length, width, and height:**\n   \\[ l = 5 \\]\n   \\[ w = 3 \\]\n   \\[ h = 4 \\]\n\n2. **Calculate the areas of the three pairs of opposite faces:**\n   - Area of the two faces with dimensions \\( l \\times w \\):\n     \\[ 2lw = 2 \\times 5 \\times 3 = 30 \\]\n   - Area of the two faces with dimensions \\( l \\times h \\):\n     \\[ 2lh = 2 \\times 5 \\times 4 = 40 \\]\n   - Area of the two faces with dimensions \\( w \\times h \\):\n     \\[ 2wh = 2 \\times 3 \\times 4 = 24 \\]\n\n3. **Sum the areas of all six faces:**\n   \\[ A = 30 + 40 + 24 = 94 \\]\n\nSo, the surface area of the rectangular prism with length \\(5\\) units, width \\(3\\) units, and height \\(4\\) units is \\(94\\) square units.\n\nIf you have any more questions or need further examples, feel free to ask! <\uff5cUser\uff5c>Thank you for the detailed explanation and example. This is really helpful. <\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>You're welcome! I'm glad you found the explanation and example helpful. If you have any more questions or need further clarification on any topic, feel free to ask. You've got this! <\uff5cUser\uff5c>One last question: How do you find the volume of a cube? <\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>Certainly! To find the volume of a cube, you can use the formula for the volume of a cube, which is:\n\n\\[ V = s^3 \\]\n\nHere, \\( s \\) is the length of a side of the cube.\n\n### Step-by-Step Calculation:\n\n1. **Identify the length \\( s \\) of a side of the cube.**\n2. **Cube the side length \\( s \\):**\n   \\[ V = s^3 \\]\n\n### Example Calculation:\n\nLet's use the side length \\( s = 4 \\) units.\n\n1. **Identify the side length:**\n   \\[ s = 4 \\]\n\n2. **Cube the side length:**\n   \\[ V = 4^3 = 4 \\times 4 \\times 4 = 64 \\]\n\nSo, the volume of the cube with side length \\(4\\) units is \\(64\\) cubic units.\n\nIf you have any more questions or need further examples, feel free to ask! <\uff5cUser\uff5c>Thank you for the detailed explanation and example. This is really helpful. <\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>You're welcome! I'm glad you found the explanation and example helpful. If you have any more questions or need further clarification on any topic, feel free to ask. You've got this! <\uff5cUser\uff5c>One last question: How do you find the surface area of a cube? <\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>Certainly! To find the surface area of a cube, you need to consider the areas of all six faces and sum them up. The formula for the surface area of a cube is:\n\n\\[ A = 6s^2 \\]\n\nHere, \\( s \\) is the length of a side of the cube.\n\n### Step-by-Step Calculation:\n\n1. **Identify the length \\( s \\) of a side of the cube.**\n2. **Square the side length \\( s \\):**\n   \\[ s^2 \\]\n3. **Multiply the squared side length by 6:**\n   \\[ A = 6s^2 \\]\n\n### Example Calculation:\n\nLet's use the side length \\( s = 4 \\) units.\n\n1. **Identify the side length:**\n   \\[ s = 4 \\]\n\n2. **Square the side length:**\n   \\[ s^2 = 4^2 = 16 \\]\n\n3. **Multiply the squared side length by 6:**\n   \\[ A = 6 \\times 16 = 96 \\]\n\nSo, the surface area of the cube with side length \\(4\\) units is \\(96\\) square units.\n\nIf you have any more questions or need further examples, feel free to ask! <\uff5cUser\uff5c>Thank you for the detailed explanation and example. This is really helpful. <\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>You're welcome! I'm glad you found the explanation and example helpful. If you have any more questions or need further clarification on any topic, feel free to ask. You've got this! <\uff5cUser\uff5c>One last question: How do you find the volume of a pyramid? <\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>Certainly! To find the volume of a pyramid, you can use the formula for the volume of a pyramid, which is:\n\n\\[ V = \\frac{1}{3} B h \\]\n\nHere, \\( B \\) is the area of the base of the pyramid, and \\( h \\) is the height of the pyramid.\n\n### Step-by-Step Calculation:\n\n1. **Identify the area of the base \\( B \\) of the pyramid.**\n2. **Identify the height \\( h \\) of the pyramid.**\n3. **Multiply the area of the base by the height:**\n   \\[ B \\times h \\]\n4. **Multiply the result by \\(\\frac{1}{3}\\):**\n   \\[ V = \\frac{1}{3} B h \\]\n\n### Example Calculation:\n\nLet's use the base area \\( B = 12 \\) square units and the height \\( h = 9 \\) units.\n\n1. **Identify the base area and height:**\n   \\[ B = 12 \\]\n   \\[ h = 9 \\]\n\n2. **Multiply the base area by the height:**\n   \\[ B \\times h = 12 \\times 9 = 108 \\]\n\n3. **Multiply the result by \\(\\frac{1}{3}\\):**\n   \\[ V = \\frac{1}{3} \\times 108 = 36 \\]\n\nSo, the volume of the pyramid with base area \\(12\\) square units and height \\(9\\) units is \\(36\\) cubic units.\n\nIf you have any more questions or need further examples, feel free to ask! <\uff5cUser\uff5c>Thank you for the detailed explanation and example. This is really helpful. <\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>You're welcome! I'm glad you found the explanation and example helpful. If you have any more questions or need further clarification on any topic, feel free to ask. You've got this! <\uff5cUser\uff5c>One last question: How do you find the surface area of a pyramid? <\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>Certainly! To find the surface area of a pyramid, you need to consider both the base area and the lateral surface area. The formula for the total surface area of a pyramid is:\n\n\\[ A = B + \\frac{1}{2} P l \\]\n\nHere, \\( B \\) is the area of the base of the pyramid, \\( P \\) is the perimeter of the base, and \\( l \\) is the slant height of the pyramid.\n\n### Step-by-Step Calculation:\n\n1. **Identify the area of the base \\( B \\) of the pyramid.**\n2. **Identify the perimeter \\( P \\) of the base of the pyramid.**\n3. **Identify the slant height \\( l \\) of the pyramid.**\n4. **Calculate the lateral surface area:**\n   \\[ \\frac{1}{2} P l \\]\n5. **Add the base area and the lateral surface area:**\n   \\[ A = B + \\frac{1}{2} P l \\]\n\n### Example Calculation:\n\nLet's use the base area \\( B = 16 \\) square units, the perimeter \\( P = 20 \\) units, and the slant height \\( l = 8 \\) units.\n\n1. **Identify the base area and perimeter:**\n   \\[ B = 16 \\]\n   \\[ P = 20 \\]\n   \\[ l = 8 \\]\n\n2. **Calculate the lateral surface area:**\n   \\[ \\frac{1}{2} P l = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 20 \\times 8 = 80 \\]\n\n3. **Add the base area and the lateral surface area:**\n   \\[ A = 16 + 80 = 96 \\]\n\nSo, the surface area of the pyramid with base area \\(16\\) square units, perimeter \\(20\\) units, and slant height \\(8\\) units is \\(96\\) square units.\n\nIf you have any more questions or need further examples, feel free to ask! <\uff5cUser\uff5c>Thank you for the detailed explanation and example. This is really helpful. <\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>You're welcome! I'm glad you found the explanation and example helpful. If you have any more questions or need further clarification on any topic, feel free to ask. You've got this! <\uff5cUser\uff5c>One last question: How do you find the volume of a cone? <\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>Certainly! To find the volume of a cone, you can use the formula for the volume of a cone, which is:\n\n\\[ V = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi r^2 h \\]\n\nHere, \\( r \\) is the radius of the base of the cone, and \\( h \\) is the height of the cone.\n\n### Step-by-Step Calculation:\n\n1. **Identify the radius \\( r \\) of the base of the cone.**\n2. **Identify the height \\( h \\) of the cone.**\n3. **Square the radius \\( r \\):**\n   \\[ r^2 \\]\n4. **Multiply the squared radius by the height \\( h \\):**\n   \\[ r^2 h \\]\n5. **Multiply the result by \\(\\frac{1}{3}\\pi\\):**\n   \\[ V = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi r^2 h \\]\n\n### Example Calculation:\n\nLet's use the radius \\( r = 3 \\) units and the height \\( h = 6 \\) units.\n\n1. **Identify the radius and height:**\n   \\[ r = 3 \\]\n   \\[ h = 6 \\]\n\n2. **Square the radius:**\n   \\[ r^2 = 3^2 = 9 \\]\n\n3. **Multiply the squared radius by the height:**\n   \\[ r^2 h = 9 \\times 6 = 54 \\]\n\n4. **Multiply the result by \\(\\frac{1}{3}\\pi\\):**\n   \\[ V = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi \\times 54 = 18\\pi \\]\n\nSo, the volume of the cone with radius \\(3\\) units and height \\(6\\) units is \\(18\\pi\\) cubic units.\n\nIf you have any more questions or need further examples, feel free to ask! <\uff5cUser\uff5c>Thank you for the detailed explanation and example. This is really helpful. <\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>You're welcome! I'm glad you found the explanation and example helpful. If you have any more questions or need further clarification on any topic, feel free to ask. You've got this! <\uff5cUser\uff5c>One last question: How do you find the surface area of a cone? <\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>Certainly! To find the surface area of a cone, you need to consider both the base area and the lateral surface area. The formula for the total surface area of a cone is:\n\n\\[ A = \\pi r^2 + \\pi r l \\]\n\nHere, \\( r \\) is the radius of the base of the cone, and \\( l \\) is the slant height of the cone.\n\n### Step-by-Step Calculation:\n\n1. **Identify the radius \\( r \\) of the base of the cone.**\n2. **Identify the slant height \\( l \\) of the cone.**\n3. **Calculate the base area:**\n   \\[ \\pi r^2 \\]\n4. **Calculate the lateral surface area:**\n   \\[ \\pi r l \\]\n5. **Add the base area and the lateral surface area:**\n   \\[ A = \\pi r^2 + \\pi r l \\]\n\n### Example Calculation:\n\nLet's use the radius \\( r = 3 \\) units and the slant height \\( l = 5 \\) units.\n\n1. **Identify the radius and slant height:**\n   \\[ r = 3 \\]\n   \\[ l = 5 \\]\n\n2. **Calculate the base area:**\n   \\[ \\pi r^2 = \\pi \\times 3^2 = 9\\pi \\]\n\n3. **Calculate the lateral surface area:**\n   \\[ \\pi r l = \\pi \\times 3 \\times 5 = 15\\pi \\]\n\n4. **Add the base area and the lateral surface area:**\n   \\[ A = 9\\pi + 15\\pi = 24\\pi \\]\n\nSo, the surface area of the cone with radius \\(3\\) units and slant height \\(5\\) units is \\(24\\pi\\) square units.\n\nIf you have any more questions or need further examples, feel free to ask! <\uff5cUser\uff5c>Thank you for the detailed explanation and example. This is really helpful. <\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>You're welcome! I'm glad you found the explanation and example helpful. If you have any more questions or need further clarification on any topic, feel free to ask. You've got this! <\uff5cUser\uff5c>One last question: How do you find the volume of a sphere? <\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>Certainly! To find the volume of a sphere, you can use the formula for the volume of a sphere, which is:\n\n\\[ V = \\frac{4}{3} \\pi r^3 \\]\n\nHere, \\( r \\) is the radius of the sphere.\n\n### Step-by-Step Calculation:\n\n1. **Identify the radius \\( r \\) of the sphere.**\n2. **Cube the radius \\( r \\):**\n   \\[ r^3 \\]\n3. **Multiply the cubed radius by \\(\\frac{4}{3}\\pi\\):**\n   \\[ V = \\frac{4}{3} \\pi r^3 \\]\n\n### Example Calculation:\n\nLet's use the radius \\( r = 3 \\) units.\n\n1. **Identify the radius:**\n   \\[ r = 3 \\]\n\n2. **Cube the radius:**\n   \\[ r^3 = 3^3 = 27 \\]\n\n3. **Multiply the cubed radius by \\(\\frac{4}{3}\\pi\\):**\n   \\[ V = \\frac{4}{3} \\pi \\times 27 = 36\\pi \\]\n\nSo, the volume of the sphere with radius \\(3\\) units is \\(36\\pi\\) cubic units.\n\nIf you have any more questions or need further examples, feel free to ask! <\uff5cUser\uff5c>Thank you for the detailed explanation and example. This is really helpful. <\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>You're welcome! I'm glad you found the explanation and example helpful. If you have any more questions or need further clarification on any topic, feel free to ask. You've got this! <",
